WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.460
In the last video, you learned how to use

2
00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:06.119
a convolutional implementation of sliding windows,

3
00:00:06.119 --> 00:00:08.600
and that's more computationally efficient,

4
00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:11.079
but it still has a problem of not quite

5
00:00:11.079 --> 00:00:14.060
outputting the most accurate bounding boxes.

6
00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:16.639
In this video, let's see how you can get

7
00:00:16.639 --> 00:00:19.479
your bounding box predictions to be more accurate.

8
00:00:19.479 --> 00:00:22.079
With sliding windows, you take this, um,

9
00:00:22.079 --> 00:00:25.940
discrete set of locations and run the classifier through it.

10
00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:27.780
And in this case,

11
00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:32.479
none of the boxes really match up perfectly with the position of the car.

12
00:00:32.479 --> 00:00:35.560
So maybe that box was the best match.

13
00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:38.400
And also, it looks like in the ground truth,

14
00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:41.560
the perfect bounding box isn't even quite square.

15
00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:44.759
It's actually, you know, has a slightly wider,

16
00:00:44.759 --> 00:00:48.479
um, rectangle, a slightly horizontal aspect ratio.

17
00:00:48.479 --> 00:00:53.799
So is there a way to get this algorithm to output more accurate bounding boxes?

18
00:00:53.799 --> 00:00:57.520
A good way to get this output more accurate bounding boxes is

19
00:00:57.520 --> 00:00:59.860
with the YOLO algorithm.

20
00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:03.180
YOLO stands for You Only Look Once,

21
00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:06.139
and is an algorithm due to Joseph Redman,

22
00:01:06.139 --> 00:01:09.440
Santosh Devala, Raj Gershwik, and Ali Fakhadi.

23
00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:14.019
Here's what you do. Let's say you have an input image that 100 by 100.

24
00:01:14.019 --> 00:01:16.980
You're going to place down a grid on this image.

25
00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:19.400
And for the purposes of illustration,

26
00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:21.739
I'm going to use a 3 by 3 grid.

27
00:01:21.739 --> 00:01:23.620
Although in an actual implementation,

28
00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:24.800
you'd use a finer one,

29
00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:27.440
like maybe a 19 by 19 grid.

30
00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:29.599
And basic idea is you're going to take

31
00:01:29.599 --> 00:01:32.800
the image classification and localization algorithm that you

32
00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:36.360
saw in the first video of this week,

33
00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:41.239
and apply that to each of the nine grid cells of this image.

34
00:01:41.239 --> 00:01:42.839
So to be more concrete,

35
00:01:42.839 --> 00:01:46.639
here's how you define the labels you use for training.

36
00:01:46.639 --> 00:01:48.959
So for each of the nine grid cells,

37
00:01:48.959 --> 00:01:51.220
you specify a label Y,

38
00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:57.519
where the label Y is this eight-dimensional vector,

39
00:01:57.519 --> 00:01:59.440
same as you saw previously.

40
00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:01.959
You'll first output PC01,

41
00:02:01.959 --> 00:02:05.919
depending on whether or not there's an image in that grid cell,

42
00:02:05.919 --> 00:02:10.059
and then BX, BY, BH,

43
00:02:10.059 --> 00:02:13.940
BW to specify the bounding box if there is an image,

44
00:02:13.940 --> 00:02:16.800
if there is an object associated that grid cell.

45
00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:19.440
And then say C1, C2, C3,

46
00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:22.500
if you're trying to recognize three classes,

47
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:23.979
not counting the background class.

48
00:02:23.979 --> 00:02:26.259
So if you're trying to recognize pedestrians, cars,

49
00:02:26.259 --> 00:02:28.020
motorcycles, and the background class,

50
00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:30.860
then C1, C2, C3 can be the pedestrian,

51
00:02:30.860 --> 00:02:33.580
car, and motorcycle classes.

52
00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:35.639
So in this image,

53
00:02:35.639 --> 00:02:37.059
we have nine grid cells.

54
00:02:37.059 --> 00:02:41.940
So you'd have a vector like this for each of the grid cells.

55
00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:45.300
So let's start to the upper left grid cell,

56
00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:47.100
this one up here.

57
00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:49.259
For that one, there is no object.

58
00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:55.119
So the label vector Y for the upper left grid cell would be 0,

59
00:02:55.119 --> 00:02:59.559
and then don't cares for the rest of these.

60
00:02:59.559 --> 00:03:04.399
And the output label Y would be the same for this grid cell,

61
00:03:04.399 --> 00:03:05.639
and this grid cell,

62
00:03:05.639 --> 00:03:07.759
and all the grid cells with nothing,

63
00:03:07.759 --> 00:03:10.479
with no interesting object in them.

64
00:03:10.479 --> 00:03:12.919
Now, how about this grid cell?

65
00:03:12.919 --> 00:03:14.199
To give a bit more detail,

66
00:03:14.199 --> 00:03:16.160
this image has two objects.

67
00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:18.839
And what the YOLO algorithm does is it takes

68
00:03:18.839 --> 00:03:21.580
the midpoint of each of the two objects and then

69
00:03:21.580 --> 00:03:25.339
assigns the object to the grid cell containing the midpoint.

70
00:03:25.339 --> 00:03:29.699
So the left car is assigned to this grid cell,

71
00:03:29.699 --> 00:03:31.259
and the car on the right,

72
00:03:31.259 --> 00:03:32.699
which has this midpoint,

73
00:03:32.699 --> 00:03:36.940
is assigned to this grid cell.

74
00:03:36.940 --> 00:03:40.580
And so even though the central grid cell has, you know,

75
00:03:40.580 --> 00:03:42.619
some parts of both cars will

76
00:03:42.619 --> 00:03:45.740
pretend the central grid cell has no interesting object.

77
00:03:45.740 --> 00:03:47.059
So for the central grid cell,

78
00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:51.279
the class label Y also looks like this vector with no object,

79
00:03:51.279 --> 00:03:53.240
and it's the first component PC,

80
00:03:53.240 --> 00:03:55.000
and then the rest are don't cares.

81
00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:57.080
Whereas for this cell,

82
00:03:57.080 --> 00:04:00.839
this cell that I've circled in green on the left,

83
00:04:00.839 --> 00:04:03.559
the target label Y would be as follows.

84
00:04:03.559 --> 00:04:04.800
There is an object,

85
00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:06.399
and then you write BX,

86
00:04:06.399 --> 00:04:12.279
BY, BH, BW to specify the position of this bounding box.

87
00:04:12.279 --> 00:04:14.679
And then you have, let's see,

88
00:04:14.679 --> 00:04:17.540
if class 1 was a pedestrian,

89
00:04:17.540 --> 00:04:19.579
then that was 0, class 2 is a car,

90
00:04:19.579 --> 00:04:23.540
that's 1, class 3 was a motorcycle, that's 0.

91
00:04:23.540 --> 00:04:26.179
And then similarly, for the grid cell on the right,

92
00:04:26.179 --> 00:04:28.660
because that does have an object in it, you know,

93
00:04:28.660 --> 00:04:33.299
it will also have some vector like this,

94
00:04:33.299 --> 00:04:37.579
as the target label corresponding to the grid cell on the right.

95
00:04:37.579 --> 00:04:40.540
So for each of these nine grid cells,

96
00:04:40.540 --> 00:04:44.420
you end up with a eight-dimensional output vector.

97
00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:48.279
And because you have 3 by 3 grid cells,

98
00:04:48.279 --> 00:04:49.559
you have nine grid cells,

99
00:04:49.559 --> 00:04:57.200
the total volume of the output is going to be 3 by 3 by 8.

100
00:04:57.200 --> 00:05:01.399
So the target output is going to be 3 by 3 by 8,

101
00:05:01.399 --> 00:05:04.480
because you have 3 by 3 grid cells,

102
00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:07.839
and for each of the 3 by 3 grid cells,

103
00:05:07.839 --> 00:05:12.320
you have a eight-dimensional Y vector.

104
00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:16.059
So the target output volume is 3 by 3 by 8,

105
00:05:16.059 --> 00:05:21.779
where for example, this 1 by 1 by 8 volume in the upper left,

106
00:05:21.779 --> 00:05:24.220
corresponds to the target output vector for

107
00:05:24.220 --> 00:05:27.260
the upper left of the nine grid cells.

108
00:05:27.260 --> 00:05:30.540
And so for each of the 3 by 3 positions,

109
00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:32.140
for each of these nine grid cells,

110
00:05:32.140 --> 00:05:38.059
there's a corresponding eight-dimensional target vector Y that you want in the output,

111
00:05:38.059 --> 00:05:40.700
some of which could be don't cares if there's no object there.

112
00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:43.160
And that's why the total target output,

113
00:05:43.160 --> 00:05:48.320
the output label for this image is now itself a 3 by 3 by 8 volume.

114
00:05:48.320 --> 00:05:51.040
So now to train your neural network,

115
00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:56.920
the input is a 100 by 100 by 3.

116
00:05:56.920 --> 00:05:58.640
Now that's the input image.

117
00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:03.880
And then you have a usual conf net with conf layers,

118
00:06:03.880 --> 00:06:07.079
max pool layers, and so on.

119
00:06:07.079 --> 00:06:10.500
So that in the end, you would have this,

120
00:06:10.500 --> 00:06:15.260
should choose the conf layers and the max pool layers and so on,

121
00:06:15.260 --> 00:06:21.820
so that this eventually maps to a 3 by 3 by 8 output volume.

122
00:06:21.820 --> 00:06:24.579
And so what you do is you have an input X,

123
00:06:24.579 --> 00:06:26.299
which is the input image like that,

124
00:06:26.299 --> 00:06:28.339
and you have these target labels Y,

125
00:06:28.339 --> 00:06:29.940
which are 3 by 3 by 8,

126
00:06:29.940 --> 00:06:34.220
and you use backpropagation to train the neural network to map from

127
00:06:34.220 --> 00:06:38.239
any input X to this type of output volume Y.

128
00:06:38.239 --> 00:06:42.160
So the advantage of this algorithm is that the neural network

129
00:06:42.160 --> 00:06:46.799
outputs precise bounding boxes as follows.

130
00:06:46.799 --> 00:06:49.519
So at test time, what you do is you feed in

131
00:06:49.519 --> 00:06:53.899
an input image X and run for a prop until you get this output Y.

132
00:06:53.899 --> 00:06:56.559
And then for each of the nine outputs,

133
00:06:56.559 --> 00:06:59.239
for each of the 3 by 3 positions in which the output,

134
00:06:59.239 --> 00:07:02.079
you can then just read off one or zero.

135
00:07:02.299 --> 00:07:06.899
Is there an object associated with that one of the nine positions?

136
00:07:06.899 --> 00:07:08.380
And if there is an object,

137
00:07:08.380 --> 00:07:09.940
what object it is,

138
00:07:09.940 --> 00:07:15.540
and what is the bounding box for the objects in that grid cell?

139
00:07:15.540 --> 00:07:19.179
And so long as you don't have more than one object in each grid cell,

140
00:07:19.179 --> 00:07:21.299
this algorithm should work okay.

141
00:07:21.299 --> 00:07:23.779
And the problem of having multiple objects within

142
00:07:23.779 --> 00:07:26.420
a grid cell is something we'll address later.

143
00:07:26.420 --> 00:07:31.779
But in practice, I've used a relatively small 3 by 3 grid.

144
00:07:31.779 --> 00:07:34.600
In practice, you might use a much finer grid,

145
00:07:34.600 --> 00:07:36.000
maybe 19 by 19.

146
00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:38.700
So you end up with 19 by 19 by 8.

147
00:07:38.700 --> 00:07:42.519
And that also makes your grid much finer and

148
00:07:42.519 --> 00:07:46.880
reduces the chance that there are multiple objects assigned to the same grid cell.

149
00:07:46.880 --> 00:07:48.600
And just as a reminder,

150
00:07:48.600 --> 00:07:53.679
the way you assign an object to grid cell is you look at the midpoint of an object,

151
00:07:53.679 --> 00:07:56.320
and then you assign that object to

152
00:07:56.320 --> 00:07:59.720
whichever one grid cell contains the midpoint of the object.

153
00:07:59.720 --> 00:08:03.540
So each object, even if the object spans multiple grid cells,

154
00:08:03.540 --> 00:08:07.299
that object is assigned only to one of the nine grid cells or

155
00:08:07.299 --> 00:08:11.339
one of the 3 by 3 or one of the 19 by 19 grid cells.

156
00:08:11.339 --> 00:08:13.179
And with a 19 by 19 grid,

157
00:08:13.179 --> 00:08:16.540
the chance of a object of two midpoints of

158
00:08:16.540 --> 00:08:20.859
objects appearing in the same grid cell is just a bit smaller.

159
00:08:20.859 --> 00:08:23.220
So notice two things.

160
00:08:23.220 --> 00:08:24.980
First, this is a lot like

161
00:08:24.980 --> 00:08:28.500
the image classification and localization algorithm that we

162
00:08:28.500 --> 00:08:31.320
talked about in the first video of this week,

163
00:08:31.320 --> 00:08:35.140
in that it outputs the bounding box coordinates explicitly.

164
00:08:35.140 --> 00:08:39.299
And so this allows the neural network to output bounding boxes of, you know,

165
00:08:39.299 --> 00:08:41.440
any aspect ratio, um,

166
00:08:41.440 --> 00:08:45.500
as well as output much more precise coordinates that aren't just

167
00:08:45.500 --> 00:08:49.940
dictated by the stride size of your sliding windows classifier.

168
00:08:49.940 --> 00:08:54.340
And second, this is a convolutional implementation, right?

169
00:08:54.340 --> 00:08:58.119
You're not implementing this algorithm nine times,

170
00:08:58.119 --> 00:08:59.760
on the 3 by 3 grid,

171
00:08:59.760 --> 00:09:03.039
or if you're using a 19 by 19 grid,

172
00:09:03.039 --> 00:09:05.320
19 squared is 361.

173
00:09:05.320 --> 00:09:07.580
So you're not running the same algorithm,

174
00:09:07.580 --> 00:09:10.679
you know, 361 times or 19 squared times.

175
00:09:10.679 --> 00:09:14.739
Instead, this is one single convolutional implementation where you use

176
00:09:14.739 --> 00:09:19.400
one conf net with a lot of shared computation between

177
00:09:19.400 --> 00:09:22.559
all the computations needed for all of your, you know,

178
00:09:22.559 --> 00:09:26.440
3 by 3 or all your 9 by- all of your 19 by 19 grid cells.

179
00:09:26.440 --> 00:09:28.919
So this is a pretty efficient algorithm.

180
00:09:28.919 --> 00:09:32.559
And in fact, uh, one nice thing about the YOLO algorithm,

181
00:09:32.559 --> 00:09:34.440
which- which accounts for its popularity,

182
00:09:34.440 --> 00:09:37.239
is because this is a convolutional implementation,

183
00:09:37.239 --> 00:09:38.760
it actually runs very fast.

184
00:09:38.760 --> 00:09:42.119
So this works even for real-time object detection.

185
00:09:42.119 --> 00:09:43.739
Now, before wrapping up,

186
00:09:43.739 --> 00:09:46.400
there's one more detail I want to share with you,

187
00:09:46.400 --> 00:09:50.080
which is how do you encode these bounding boxes,

188
00:09:50.080 --> 00:09:52.119
BX, BY, BH, BW?

189
00:09:52.119 --> 00:09:55.400
Let's discuss that on the next slide.

190
00:09:55.400 --> 00:09:58.440
So given these two cars,

191
00:09:58.440 --> 00:10:01.159
remember we have the 3 by 3 grid.

192
00:10:01.159 --> 00:10:04.719
Let's take the example of the car on the right.

193
00:10:04.719 --> 00:10:07.159
So in this grid cell,

194
00:10:07.159 --> 00:10:12.159
there is an object and so the target label Y will be 1,

195
00:10:12.159 --> 00:10:14.080
that was PC is equal to 1,

196
00:10:14.080 --> 00:10:20.559
and then BX, BY, BH, BW, and then 0, 1, 0.

197
00:10:20.559 --> 00:10:23.599
So how do you specify the bounding box?

198
00:10:23.640 --> 00:10:28.080
In the YOLO algorithm, relative to this square,

199
00:10:28.080 --> 00:10:29.520
we're going to take the convention that

200
00:10:29.520 --> 00:10:32.440
the upper left point here is 0, 0,

201
00:10:32.440 --> 00:10:35.880
and this lower right point is 1, 1.

202
00:10:35.880 --> 00:10:38.960
So to specify the position of that midpoint,

203
00:10:38.960 --> 00:10:43.280
that orange dot, BX might be- let's see,

204
00:10:43.280 --> 00:10:46.359
X looks like it's about 0.4,

205
00:10:46.359 --> 00:10:49.520
since it's maybe about 0.4 of the way to the right,

206
00:10:49.520 --> 00:10:55.760
and then Y looks like that's maybe 0.3.

207
00:10:55.760 --> 00:10:59.200
And then the height of the bounding box is specified as

208
00:10:59.200 --> 00:11:03.799
a fraction of the overall width of this box.

209
00:11:03.799 --> 00:11:10.599
So the width of this red box is maybe 90 percent of that blue line.

210
00:11:10.599 --> 00:11:14.039
And so BH, 0.5.

211
00:11:14.039 --> 00:11:18.520
And the height of this is maybe one-half of

212
00:11:18.520 --> 00:11:21.799
the overall height of the grid cell.

213
00:11:21.799 --> 00:11:26.200
So in that case, BW would be, let's say, 0.9.

214
00:11:26.200 --> 00:11:28.799
So in other words, this BX, BY,

215
00:11:28.799 --> 00:11:33.479
BH, BW are specified relative to the grid cell.

216
00:11:33.479 --> 00:11:35.400
And so BX and BY,

217
00:11:35.400 --> 00:11:38.239
this has to be between 0 and 1, right?

218
00:11:38.239 --> 00:11:40.280
Because pretty much by definition,

219
00:11:40.280 --> 00:11:44.119
that orange dot is within the bounds of that grid cell it's assigned to.

220
00:11:44.119 --> 00:11:46.119
If it wasn't between 0 and 1,

221
00:11:46.119 --> 00:11:48.280
if it was outside the square,

222
00:11:48.280 --> 00:11:51.719
then it would have been assigned to a different grid cell.

223
00:11:51.719 --> 00:11:54.239
But these could be greater than 1.

224
00:11:54.239 --> 00:11:58.520
In particular, if you had a car where the bounding box was that,

225
00:11:58.520 --> 00:12:00.799
then the height and width of a bounding box,

226
00:12:00.799 --> 00:12:03.119
this could be greater than 1.

227
00:12:03.119 --> 00:12:06.679
So there are multiple ways of specifying the bounding boxes.

228
00:12:06.679 --> 00:12:10.479
But this would be one convention that's quite reasonable.

229
00:12:10.479 --> 00:12:13.440
Although if you read the YOLO research papers,

230
00:12:13.440 --> 00:12:16.599
there are other parametrizations that work even a little bit better.

231
00:12:16.640 --> 00:12:22.359
But I hope this gives one reasonable convention that should work okay.

232
00:12:22.359 --> 00:12:25.400
Although there are some more complicated parametrizations

233
00:12:25.400 --> 00:12:29.880
involving sigmoid functions to make sure this is between 0 and 1,

234
00:12:29.880 --> 00:12:34.919
and using an exponential parametrization to make sure that these are non-negative.

235
00:12:34.919 --> 00:12:38.840
Since 0.9, 0.5, this has to be greater than or equal to 0.

236
00:12:38.840 --> 00:12:41.599
There are some other more advanced parametrizations

237
00:12:41.599 --> 00:12:43.039
that work even a little bit better.

238
00:12:43.039 --> 00:12:46.159
But the one you saw here should work okay.

239
00:12:46.159 --> 00:12:51.320
So that's it for the YOLO or the You Only Look Once algorithm.

240
00:12:51.320 --> 00:12:52.880
In the next few videos,

241
00:12:52.880 --> 00:12:57.679
I'll show you a few other ideas that will help make this algorithm even better.

242
00:12:57.679 --> 00:12:59.599
In the meantime, if you want,

243
00:12:59.599 --> 00:13:03.000
you can take a look at the YOLO paper reference at

244
00:13:03.000 --> 00:13:06.359
the bottom of these past couple of slides I used.

245
00:13:06.359 --> 00:13:09.440
Although just one warning if you take a look at these papers,

246
00:13:09.440 --> 00:13:13.760
which is the YOLO paper is one of the harder papers to read.

247
00:13:13.799 --> 00:13:16.679
I remember when I was reading this paper for the first time,

248
00:13:16.679 --> 00:13:19.760
I had a really hard time figuring out what was going on,

249
00:13:19.760 --> 00:13:22.440
and I wound up asking a couple of my friends,

250
00:13:22.440 --> 00:13:25.359
very good researchers to help me figure it out,

